  "image_name": "5bf9de83cecfd9ede734ec7fd10f7a5d.png",
  "prompt_text": "This sagittal brain schematic summarizes how hypothalamic AgRP and POMC neurons project to many feeding- and autonomic-related regions, and how their targets differ in melanocortin receptor expression. Cell bodies for AgRP and POMC appear clustered in the arcuate nucleus (ARH), from which long axons extend widely through the forebrain, midbrain, and hindbrain. The projections reach areas such as the paraventricular hypothalamus, lateral hypothalamus, dorsomedial and ventromedial hypothalamus, bed nucleus of the stria terminalis, lateral septum, ventral tegmental area, parabrachial nucleus, periaqueductal gray, dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus, and broad reticular nuclei.\n\nThe color coding emphasizes receptor distribution at these target sites: some regions mainly show MC4R, others MC3R, and several contain both. Many prominent hypothalamic and brainstem targets appear to express both receptor types, suggesting overlapping melanocortin signaling across circuits that regulate energy balance and motivated behavior. The two projection maps largely overlap, indicating that AgRP and POMC neurons influence many of the same structures, though exact branching patterns differ. Overall, the figure conveys a distributed melanocortin network linking the arcuate nucleus to numerous downstream centers involved in feeding, arousal, reward, and autonomic control.",
